    "result_text": "Done. Build is green.\n\n## What I changed\n\nThe de-duplication rule is implemented as a new, self-contained pipeline step rather than by modifying `AssignCustomerCode`.\n\n1. **New function class** `DeduplicateCustomerCode.java` \u2014 one small method (`service`). It collects existing owners' `customerCode`s, and if the freshly-computed code collides, appends `-<n>` starting at `n=2`, incrementing until unique, then writes it back onto the owner.\n\n2. **Wired it into `owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 inserted a `deduplicateCustomerCode` step between `assignCustomerCode` and `assignNamesakeCount`, under the same `transaction` governance. It runs before `save`, so `findAll()` only sees pre-existing owners.\n\n## Why this keeps structural impact low\n\n- `AssignCustomerCode` (which carries the `hash8` helper as `WMC_other`) is untouched \u2014 I avoided paying for its surrounding complexity.\n- The new behaviour lives in a fresh class with a single method: `WMC_other = 0` and low CC, so its per-function cost is minimal.\n- Only two files touched (the new class + the YAML), and no existing function's body grew.\n\nThe `DeduplicateCustomerCode` step mirrors the existing `AssignNamesakeCount`/`EnsureUniqueHousehold` pattern (scan `findAll()`, act before save), so it reads like its neighbors. The acceptance test's guarantee \u2014 distinct owners always get distinct `customerCode`s \u2014 and the full suite both pass.",
